From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mower <mdmower@cmphys.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:03:36 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [bugs #3701] Accept a second declaration of an identifier
 with linkage in a block

addSymChain() rejected every declaration that named an identifier of
the same block again, with error 91 when one of the two declarations
was extern. C11 6.7 paragraph 3 limits identifiers without linkage to
one declaration in a scope. Objects that are declared extern and
functions have linkage, so

    void f (void)
    {
      extern const uint8_t * const name_data[];
      extern const uint8_t * const name_data[];
    }

is valid.

addSymChain() now treats two such declarations in a block like two
declarations at file scope. It completes the array length and the
prototype in both types and compares the types. The symbol of the
first declaration stays in the symbol table, because resolveSymbols()
finds the symbol for an expression by level, block, and sequence
point, and expressions between the two declarations need it.
addSymChain() takes the symbol of the second declaration out of the
chain. The actions of the two declaration_list rules now add the chain
to the list of the block after that call. A second declaration that
has an initializer gets error 91 as before.

addSymChain() read the extern flag from the first link of the type
when it tested for a visible declaration without linkage. That link
has the flag only when the type does not have a declarator, so a
block-scope extern declaration of an array, a pointer, or a function
counted as a declaration without linkage. addSymChain() then compared
the type of a further extern declaration exactly with the type at file
scope. For

    int a[2];

    int f (void)
    {
      extern int a[];
      {
        extern int a[];
        return a[1];
      }
    }

the inner declaration got error 91, and so did a second declaration
in the block of the first one. The flag is now read from the
specifier.

Adds tests/bug-3701.c to the regression suite and to valdiag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
